package "main"
import "pkg/builtins/testing"

import "pkg/fvcb"

// The TRUE cross-package litmus for PIECE 2 (arm32 option-B by-address non-coerced
// aggregate func-value args) at the boundary that actually matters:
//   native-DISPATCH -> LLVM-EMITTED-shim.
//
// Native `main` OBTAINS a *func value from the LLVM-compiled dep pkg/fvcb
// (GetSliceCallback) WITHOUT taking any function's address itself, then DISPATCHES
// it (cb(...)).  Because the func value is CREATED in the dep (the dep takes
// &sumSlice), the per-function func-value shim (`@__shim.<mangled>`) is emitted BY
// THE DEP under LLVM, and the vtable.call slot points at THAT dep shim.  main emits
// NO shim for sumSlice (it never names or address-takes it), so main's
// OP_CALL_FUNC_VALUE dispatch runs native emitCallFuncValue and jumps through the
// vtable straight into the DEP's LLVM shim.  This is the boundary option B's
// correctness rests on.
//
// Contrast 1003 (slice, SAME package): there `main` takes the address itself
// (var f = fvslice.Sum), so main builds its OWN native shim, and the dispatch reaches
// a NATIVE shim, then that native shim tail-branches to the LLVM callee.  1003 never
// exercises native-dispatch -> LLVM-EMITTED-shim.  1006 does: the DEP's LLVM shim's
// by-address (i8*) contract is FIXED, so a wrong native-dispatch marshal is a hard
// miscompile here.
//
// The SLICE arg (*[]int, 8B, a NON-coerced 2-word in-register aggregate) is the
// genuinely-non-coerced shape PIECE 2 added: isByAddressAggArm32 TRUE.  The LLVM
// shim declares it i8* (by-address); native dispatch must pass ONE pointer word.  An
// option-A inline 2-word spread would desync -> wrong sum.  (Mutation-verified:
// forcing the slice inline in the dispatch marshal crashes, proving the by-address
// boundary is genuinely exercised.)  A coerced aggregate — array / named struct /
// source-level anon struct — rides the pre-existing by-address coerced path and adds
// no new boundary coverage, so this litmus is slice-only.

func main() {
	// SLICE arg: 10 + 20 + 3 + 9 == 42.  By-address on both sides.
	var arr [4]int = [4]int{10, 20, 3, 9}
	var cb *func(*[]int) int = fvcb.GetSliceCallback()
	testing.Println(cb(arr[:])) // 42
}
